#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171905 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172099 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "629dd9d1-1c53-5a04-97bf-5799e71b9573" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Other Type I and Type II Regions"}] :question/id #uuid "1c03777d-2ead-5333-bf75-0e9a96fdb0e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9563" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle -3xy^2 , \\: y+\\cos x \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0), (2,2),$ and $(-2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/aa/aa822f0b9259d6d9eb5c6f439588e70d4b9bee16b2b868fae36c7cbac72d3c3f.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y) = -3xy^2$ and $Q(x,y) = y+\\cos x .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) :  -y \\leq x \\leq y, \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-3{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}(-3{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{y}^{2}+1)[x]_{-y}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{y}^{2}+1)(y-(-y))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{y}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2y\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-6{y}^{3}+2y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[-6\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}+2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{3{y}^{4}}{2}+{y}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(-24+4)-0 \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171906 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172100 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5c7e707-c978-566d-8a69-98c84c7eb53e" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem in Flux Form"}] :question/id #uuid "a9d748d6-d688-5b77-a250-cb534a371765" :question/math-academy-id "e-9564" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0), (-1,-3), (1,-3),$ and $(1,0),$  traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (7x^2y^6 +x^3)\\, \\mathbf{i} + (- 2xy^7+4x^2y) \\, \\mathbf{j}$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/02/02964b07e45daba2d560fa16d34bcc92ddb8d0e0e83f644f8284dbe9d4b4557b.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y) =  7x^2y^6 +x^3$ and $Q(x,y) =- 2xy^7+4x^2y.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=14x{y}^{6}+3{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-14x{y}^{6}+4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y) :  -1\\leq x\\leq 1, \\, -3\\leq y \\leq 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(14x{y}^{6}+3{x}^{2})+(-14x{y}^{6}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-3}^{0}7{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171907 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172101 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/id #uuid "ed1dfb37-d01e-5d35-82f2-a441cdd54f3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9565" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle \\sin(\\pi y) - x , \\: \\cos(\\pi x) + 2xy \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square with vertices $(0,-1), (2,-1), (2,1),$ and $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/2f/2f3374de4a29c62587a79f6286dfe788e64c37e09e9d9b83b3a67b4dac10dc21.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y) = \\sin(\\pi y) - x$ and $Q(x,y) = \\cos(\\pi x) + 2xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) :  0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\, -1 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}-x)\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({2}^{2}-2)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\,dy \\\\  & =2y\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2(1)-2(-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172100 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5c7e707-c978-566d-8a69-98c84c7eb53e" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem in Flux Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cb0875b-4ef8-58eb-8217-9c53fb18ec9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}-5y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260529} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8d79b2-c27e-5bcf-9329-f7f7ae12d537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+5y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261120} {:answer/id #uuid "9f228826-f550-52dc-83d1-70ff2e48a178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}x(2y+5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261255} {:answer/id #uuid "c8cc4bc7-93b1-5f30-8801-7d45f0f67ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2y+5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261649} {:answer/id #uuid "dd26827a-6b30-5805-9ec2-35c779e0f6bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}x(2y-5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8cc4bc7-93b1-5f30-8801-7d45f0f67ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2y+5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261649} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1a2ac70-d4cf-5928-8804-e1eb884d3787" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bab01488-68e9-5dde-9b92-c15b0288a3e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-127072" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,2),$ and $(-1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\,i+5xy\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/26/265d27ad87a7f114bd2a32a5f48cbfedb13192abf48f9b759c4ed635e71ff144.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=5xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+5x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2y+5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172100 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5c7e707-c978-566d-8a69-98c84c7eb53e" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem in Flux Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25758b2a-af8a-5a90-81ab-acfe72d274c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260201} {:answer/id #uuid "66ae0255-9a5f-52f4-acb5-19660b53dbce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260769} {:answer/id #uuid "904beab1-505f-5e48-a793-2b0d5eec535a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261129} {:answer/id #uuid "d53f3519-e121-50e5-9ddf-3dbe90831402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261752} {:answer/id #uuid "e7611642-0621-5c4d-9835-a60ea38e0d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66ae0255-9a5f-52f4-acb5-19660b53dbce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260769} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b4f3e47-9d1d-52ec-bb38-4c32ab7f1c29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8fd9f34-b5c0-5dde-8e43-965eb6f71e26" :question/math-academy-id "q-127206" :question/problem "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$F(x,y)=⟨y+2{x}^{2}y,\\,{x}^{2}-x{y}^{2}⟩$\nacross the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and  $(0,1)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/98/98d7da0c7cd1e1f1006c1db74ae1a1e1f198d9066df6585745ee36548f0b8d96.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y+2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}-x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy+(-2xy))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2xy.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172100 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5c7e707-c978-566d-8a69-98c84c7eb53e" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem in Flux Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "209d3320-50d9-50ef-9a25-a823b828f588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}-3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424447} {:answer/id #uuid "3eed97bf-f19a-5ae8-9a9c-33659aaf6987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424454} {:answer/id #uuid "5c52dcea-70d7-560b-8792-445eff50c6a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424459} {:answer/id #uuid "8cceeb19-30be-531c-aaa0-0ea7e33dd634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424474} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf9c71a-e752-5728-b94c-b39be38cdb47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}+2{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cceeb19-30be-531c-aaa0-0ea7e33dd634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424474} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8f6f773-72e6-553b-a49d-36c27b57974f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fec27738-a1e3-5883-83b7-8672ecd1a476" :question/math-academy-id "q-127224" :question/problem "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(0,-2),$ and $(1,-1)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f1/f1b9472021d3973167a7601785803837627476a4e8b1460e42f87e91ecb99d7e.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+{y}^{3}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2x+3{y}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172100 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5c7e707-c978-566d-8a69-98c84c7eb53e" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem in Flux Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6929e9f0-853a-5eae-90fd-c61e9acb4821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5({x}^{3}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424463} {:answer/id #uuid "8581b009-276f-5642-a422-5b78da828fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424471} {:answer/id #uuid "89bd431e-528a-55dc-b640-1fbdebadacfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424473} {:answer/id #uuid "be2b7276-0419-561c-a09e-7c016dde655a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424485} {:answer/id #uuid "fad12fdf-85ee-5681-8fee-a7e21e160d4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({x}^{3}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6929e9f0-853a-5eae-90fd-c61e9acb4821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5({x}^{3}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424463} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f093a1f-fd70-5b60-bb5c-a3a70624352c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec31d0f0-f633-5965-938b-954592f5e7bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-127226" :question/problem "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{4}+{y}^{3},\\,({x}^{3}+5)y⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(1,0),(2,0),$ and $(0,2)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/63/636bfd79297e6fef4b0e56af9b0cb22f2e9a8061e833ea1dcf07e7958b870cdc.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{4}+{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=({x}^{3}+5)y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4{x}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & ={x}^{3}+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}+5)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5({x}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=5({x}^{3}+1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172100 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5c7e707-c978-566d-8a69-98c84c7eb53e" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem in Flux Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1844ee7-71f8-5f82-b00f-48f60255736b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{-1}{\\int }_{-3}^{1}5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261279} {:answer/id #uuid "af686067-c1f0-550a-9035-c1db108598e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261423} {:answer/id #uuid "b9f7ac06-c16a-56ad-828e-96b57398b8a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{3}(2xy+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261505} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e73e44-365c-517b-be69-1b06c081287f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{-1}{\\int }_{-3}^{1}(5{x}^{3}+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261576} {:answer/id #uuid "e07c2725-c3eb-5417-b03a-c1bf16f00f91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{y}^{3}(x-5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af686067-c1f0-550a-9035-c1db108598e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261423} :answer-field/id #uuid "59ffff1c-063b-5515-bce4-0632652300f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25b638ef-851a-5e05-bca0-4d63723c7d75" :question/math-academy-id "q-127231" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-3,-1),(-1,-1),(-1,1),$ and $(-3,1),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)=(y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}x)\\,i+(-{y}^{2}x+{y}^{4})\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/54/5497f23e51d1e745fef1cf39d6c1a8ada22b25e8c9c7b87f5d5338e85a941739.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}x$ and $Q(x,y)=-{y}^{2}x+{y}^{4}.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2yx+{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2yx+4{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-3\\le x\\le -1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2yx+{y}^{3})+(-2yx+4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172101 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60eea807-02ce-5996-b952-62ec842109d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424461} {:answer/id #uuid "799a4193-8f0b-5d08-a4bf-2ba532ac8bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424468} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd7d3e5-183d-50b2-bbd9-75b14c583ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424469} {:answer/id #uuid "974a14ee-cedf-5300-8d94-d8f4a66e99c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424475} {:answer/id #uuid "fbeea2e4-21f9-5c63-bc1c-d0e42e6ef742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbeea2e4-21f9-5c63-bc1c-d0e42e6ef742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424502} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd4aac36-d301-5889-827c-4b73f4384b55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef2a7e7d-9054-51ec-bb29-4e186ac65a15" :question/math-academy-id "q-127307" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{y}^{2}+8x{y}^{3},\\,{e}^{x}+2{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $-1\\le x\\le 0,0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/bb/bb0033d2b275442ee92cc0b805c71102641c670ea1b0edba97d31a9262795c83.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}+8x{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{x}+2{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=8{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\,x\\,{|}_{-1}^{0}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\cdot 1\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{3}+4y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[2{y}^{4}+2{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2(1)^{4}+2(1)^{2})-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262754 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172099 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "629dd9d1-1c53-5a04-97bf-5799e71b9573" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Other Type I and Type II Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "409e352b-1dfe-5ff9-aedc-75328dd5c0e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260440} {:answer/id #uuid "4152d260-9884-52bb-8f86-bc9609db7be7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260446} {:answer/id #uuid "42d0fbc3-b082-5c28-ad06-10f183c57616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260456} {:answer/id #uuid "66b1bfdb-4012-5b43-9ff0-5d28b334ab44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260770} {:answer/id #uuid "eca1aa3c-6ff5-5fcb-bf3c-315c4ea87127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{50}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42d0fbc3-b082-5c28-ad06-10f183c57616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260456} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f348574-a376-5e8c-90e1-01f7851d5439" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d270431a-39a1-5ea3-a306-51d0cd90b607" :question/math-academy-id "q-127309" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨6y{x}^{2}+{e}^{y},\\,3{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6e80e3010ec7c40d3f59dbb1da4e77c985c889a720b6f954177d8ad83a01351.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6y{x}^{2}+{e}^{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=3{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=12yx,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(12yx+6y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}(12yx+6y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{y}^{2}x+3{y}^{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{x}^{2}+3x)\\,dx \\\\  & =(2{x}^{3}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =22-0 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172099 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "629dd9d1-1c53-5a04-97bf-5799e71b9573" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Other Type I and Type II Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdeb3565-33b8-5e33-8e62-a379ccee15a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261551} {:answer/id #uuid "d61001de-9748-5fd5-a503-22e24e85614b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261757} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b6bc8d-1963-572f-81c7-31d894805c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261765} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2e40f2-95b7-5d9f-a3c0-5505414cee4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261946} {:answer/id #uuid "fbfa1d83-7d56-5a32-8910-1d3f25e76d97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d61001de-9748-5fd5-a503-22e24e85614b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261757} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee924e6b-2ed7-5385-812d-4d3eda3ed721" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1199c00-6bec-5b54-8e65-8c9cf313406e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127316" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨x-\\sqrt{y},\\,y-\\sqrt{x}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the line $y=0$ for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/c4/c4dd4d03bdfcf6c3d597e2fdef8681e4f3316b3f276cb9967933b61b0dc90976.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x-\\sqrt{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=y-\\sqrt{x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,dx \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =2(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(0-(-1)) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262757 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172101 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08ef51b5-bef5-548c-8f51-a79f305af85b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259957} {:answer/id #uuid "80081f79-d17b-5546-ab52-2540982bd374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260986} {:answer/id #uuid "806a70e6-fd99-58c6-a425-5c06d2336cd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260991} {:answer/id #uuid "ef1969e2-dc11-53e4-a52a-96db98443bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261977} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc96c67-0441-581c-839f-b9e98cb25a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef1969e2-dc11-53e4-a52a-96db98443bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261977} :answer-field/id #uuid "0261ce20-dc45-5b39-aeaf-012d29e230ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5702cf64-a04c-533d-bb19-d90253b29fec" :question/math-academy-id "q-127322" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\sqrt[3]{y}+x,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/ea6c25e40b6683d9ff4a4884dbbda708fd79dbd79d0b50730f9ad6e165d2eb58.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{y}+x$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-6{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(1-6{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-2{x}^{3}y)\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(2-2(2)^{3}y)-0]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-16y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[2y-8{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2(1)-8(1)^{2})-0 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172099 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "629dd9d1-1c53-5a04-97bf-5799e71b9573" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Other Type I and Type II Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23fba3b8-2900-5090-8e9f-03e7367e09eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260188} {:answer/id #uuid "2e6f3730-903b-591c-86aa-1010845f9300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260293} {:answer/id #uuid "4758297b-9cdb-529a-852e-7fbfc833d22a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260495} {:answer/id #uuid "51730068-71f9-59da-a522-e4eeca4b1d91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260565} {:answer/id #uuid "e89161a2-7639-5667-9c16-f59bf41e64af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51730068-71f9-59da-a522-e4eeca4b1d91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260565} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e5dc6e5-2148-538c-b14d-fe9cfe804e13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "235350dc-4663-567f-92d6-1215c91e7efd" :question/math-academy-id "q-127323" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨6{x}^{2}+2y,\\,-10y-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0),(2,1),$ and $(-2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/3a/3ad0a876fe801980a2c3724a639fc6c379db750635c0278a10a5094e2d64c34a.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6{x}^{2}+2y$ and $Q(x,y)=-10y-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=12x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-2y\\le x\\le 2y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(12x-10)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2y}^{2y}(12x-10)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[6{x}^{2}-10x]_{-2y}^{2y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(24{y}^{2}-20y)-(24{y}^{2}+20y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-40y)\\,dy \\\\  & =-40{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-40(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172101 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d2058c9-6db4-581f-a065-7bb1cf07c556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260123} {:answer/id #uuid "2b8b210e-ceef-5d7e-a20b-9a79b427549e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260260} {:answer/id #uuid "be97a3e0-af5b-55f6-8a9d-668875533062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261556} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa02f2a-4b5c-5f74-9d43-bc4fc65096f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261568} {:answer/id #uuid "cacc2a97-a4f6-538c-9ceb-91985ed7b814" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfa02f2a-4b5c-5f74-9d43-bc4fc65096f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261568} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d22bdec-d0fd-5742-b103-7ec2dae7bac3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d93ec47e-8065-5376-b8dd-7b5786ea175a" :question/math-academy-id "q-127324" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)-y{x}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2xy⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square with vertices $(0,-1),(1,-1),(1,0),$ and $(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/34/3497b3bbc834879026235f97468f5feb26e91a2b73a4de3da87597bf422ecd33.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)-y{x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2xy+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x-2xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{2}-{x}^{2}y)\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[(1-y)-0]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(1-y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-((-1)-\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172099 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "629dd9d1-1c53-5a04-97bf-5799e71b9573" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Other Type I and Type II Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c19fbd3-eb95-539d-bf87-676882069ed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260268} {:answer/id #uuid "6abfd50c-9e0e-5020-a336-2ba73402223b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260811} {:answer/id #uuid "8ffe60d9-b0ff-56b4-841a-a0c93b74cf46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261127} {:answer/id #uuid "b5bfec2e-6d9e-56cc-b6d7-29dec1dfb273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261471} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f4d10d-23f5-5225-a928-01986a2f242c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6abfd50c-9e0e-5020-a336-2ba73402223b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260811} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa559da7-dc21-5a7b-ab0a-f489b126cb88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0a994fc8-218e-56c0-8e85-b472da8df87e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127325" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨xy-\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1},\\,{x}^{3}+y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0),(1,2),$ and $(-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/00/006d80a7dbe4d03b78cc5fb1a642405e3fdfdd69ec9d80014ee8e0355ad1ae73.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=xy-\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{y}{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y/2}^{y/2}(y+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+1)[x]_{-y/2}^{y/2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+1)[\\frac{y}{2}-(-\\frac{y}{2})]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}+y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{4}{2})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172101 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42f7866a-874a-59e8-98df-e82497daf28c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540642} {:answer/id #uuid "44289665-fb34-50eb-942c-684dbafac462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540644} {:answer/id #uuid "5d52696f-f66a-5483-9d31-ad3010ca9bf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540685} {:answer/id #uuid "675393a7-a22d-52c0-8122-89d84461848c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540704} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1a18cf-57e7-594a-bab2-ece7b5646c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b1a18cf-57e7-594a-bab2-ece7b5646c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540734} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcc96fea-c51a-5b5e-9f54-45e9c86734fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "512baac6-6af2-5983-be4f-040d5e0743f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-127327" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y{x}^{3},\\,\\sqrt[3]{x}+y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square with vertices $(-1,0),(1,0),(1,2),$ and $(-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/fa/fa98f3db31860a463d0a551d5297895e61a6c041d852c04de04c44a194510961.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y{x}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{x}+y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}y+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3{x}^{2}y+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}y+x)\\,{|}_{-1}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[((1)^{3}y+1)-((-1)^{3}y+(-1))]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2y+2)\\,dy \\\\  & =[{y}^{2}+2y]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =((2)^{2}+2(2))-0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172099 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "629dd9d1-1c53-5a04-97bf-5799e71b9573" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Other Type I and Type II Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3452acaf-89b6-53ae-861d-c8309d517e98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424451} {:answer/id #uuid "38444180-4e68-5f1d-94e7-63374144fcf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424452} {:answer/id #uuid "395d167e-4282-5829-b24d-6655ea33a853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424453} {:answer/id #uuid "4b779ef3-0c88-57d7-943a-271c2a83d88c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424456} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf01d52-6bde-5d5c-8f28-24beb09eed98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbf01d52-6bde-5d5c-8f28-24beb09eed98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424491} :answer-field/id #uuid "19474be3-1fd4-58b1-9c1c-f483db0b1748" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "44ac1bd4-9843-582f-80a5-1b691b1d5da4" :question/math-academy-id "q-127328" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+y,\\,xy+1⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=-y,$ $\\,x=\\sqrt{y},$ and the line $y=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/2e/2ecabe22395006f0c7ef0a3f914087fa324f4d59c7d7886c18907f824744643e.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$ and $Q(x,y)=xy+1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-y\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{\\sqrt{y}}3x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-y}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424517 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172101 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1afc0e4b-7ec3-5e04-a320-b09bbb7450d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424446} {:answer/id #uuid "5ebdbb3a-9cd8-57c7-9768-3437a54bb5b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424460} {:answer/id #uuid "783926e4-15e5-52ff-ad59-711ade6b214d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424467} {:answer/id #uuid "9ceb053a-ae1f-53f2-9912-d9d9157552fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424476} {:answer/id #uuid "db270bba-95df-51f5-b9d1-ccab96038132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "783926e4-15e5-52ff-ad59-711ade6b214d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424467} :answer-field/id #uuid "15f3b20c-fcac-5124-a093-6cfd6cef919f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5645d046-8b12-5960-a3e0-e0429413dd27" :question/math-academy-id "q-127337" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{y}^{7}-x,\\,{e}^{3x}+x{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1,-1),(0,-1),(0,1),$ and $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b25d12781632bcc03576a79e7fbf1b22806fc68e3f521ecd602f1b0732d8d90d.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{7}-x$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{3x}+x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2xy-1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}y-x)\\,{|}_{-1}^{0}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[0-((-1)^{2}y-(-1))]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-y-1)\\,dy \\\\  & =[-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-y]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}-(1))-(-\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}-(-1)) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
